6176618 #edb/ref 1061 148507 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176618 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "506efb66-4bf2-5671-a2d5-924909de20b3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176618 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186175910 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176619 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186176620 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176619 #edb/ref 1061 148511 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176619 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "aa238b15-2e50-58d7-a7ee-fa21c5e972ae" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176619 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186175911 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176620 #edb/ref 1061 148514 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176620 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "54b30d3f-b82a-541d-946f-a14a88996ec5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176620 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186175912 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177096 #edb/ref 1064 "The graph of\n\n$$\n{y=2x+7}\n$$\n\nis shown in the picture below. Let's find the equation of the line that passes through $(4,-6)$ and is parallel to\n\n$$\ny=2x+7\n$$\n\n![](images/d8/d80d162b46c39cebddf07394219ec15f4bdbda94831cd66d22da8f285b680d61.png)\n\nSince parallel lines have equal slopes, the slope of the new line should be equal to the slope of\n\n$$\ny=2x+7\n$$\n\nwhich is $2$.\n\nTherefore, we wish to find a line that has a slope of $2$ and passes through the point $(4,-6)$. To find the equation of this line, we use the point-slope form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny - y_{1} &= m(x - x_{1}) \\\\\ny - (-6) &= 2(x - 4) \\\\\ny + 6 &= 2x - 8 \\\\\ny &= 2x - 14\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177096 #edb/ref 1065 8901 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177096 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "2e8f95a3-bef7-580d-877c-269f8df50256" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177096 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481500 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481533 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186481500 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186481500 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/f9/f97a0ccabbbdd6db1b331deea33c80ab24f3beceb15754ac22d70e8aafb17b0c.png)\n\nThe equation of the line $l_{1}$ is $y =-\\frac{2}{3}x - \\frac{7}{3}$ and the line $l_{2}$ is parallel to $l_{1}$. What is the equation of $l_{2}$?$y =$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186481500 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "126edf55-54fb-5122-b14c-923ccea5da62" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186481500 #edb/ref 1074 "Since parallel lines have equal slopes, the slope of the new line should be equal to the slope of the line\n\n$$\ny = -\\frac{2}{3}x - \\frac{7}{3}\n$$\n\nThe slope of the line\n\n$$\ny = -\\frac{2}{3}x - \\frac{7}{3}\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\n-\\frac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nSo, we want a line with the slope $-\\frac{2}{3}$ that passes through the point $(-2,-3)$. Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny - y_{1} &= m(x - x_{1}) \\\\\ny - (-3) &= -\\frac{2}{3}(x - (-2)) \\\\\ny &= -\\frac{2}{3}x - \\frac{4}{3} + (-3) \\\\\ny &= -\\frac{2}{3}x - \\frac{13}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186481504 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481499 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186481504 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186481504 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/ea/eaa82463dcdaad6bc1616b77c249a097483a8ca10afeda4bd7673896514b9efa.png)\n\nThe equation of the line $l_{1}$ is $y = -3x + 1$ and the line $l_{2}$ is parallel to $l_{1}$. What is the equation of $l_{2}$?" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186481504 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2a4b8abf-f619-531a-a8b5-8e24db57d662" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186481504 #edb/ref 1074 "Since parallel lines have equal slopes, the slope of the new line should be equal to the slope of the line\n\n$$\ny = -3x + 1\n$$\n\nThe slope of the line\n\n$$\ny = -3x + 1\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\n-3\n$$\n\nSo, we want a line with the slope $-3$ that passes through the point $(0,-1)$. Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny - y_{1} &= m(x - x_{1}) \\\\\ny - (-1) &= -3(x - (0)) \\\\\ny &= -3x + (-1) \\\\\ny &= -3x - 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186481505 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481523 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186481505 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186481505 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/5b/5baf024303863c69279bd1fb606a2bc8609dae35d5c5e1a9a998e9defaa0c621.png)\n\nThe equation of the line $l_{1}$ is $y = 2x - 3$ and the line $l_{2}$ is parallel to $l_{1}$. What is the equation of $l_{2}$?" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186481505 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2b4f4747-b081-583d-87f5-e1f89a2c834b" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186481505 #edb/ref 1074 "Since parallel lines have equal slopes, the slope of the new line should be equal to the slope of the line\n\n$$\ny = 2x - 3\n$$\n\nThe slope of the line\n\n$$\ny = 2x - 3\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\n2\n$$\n\nSo, we want a line with the slope $2$ that passes through the point $(0,-5)$. Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny - y_{1} &= m(x - x_{1}) \\\\\ny - (-5) &= 2(x - (0)) \\\\\ny &= 2x + (-5) \\\\\ny &= 2x - 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186481509 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481530 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186481509 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186481509 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/b4/b4951f9f511e3ddec793750a6562f20c5aed6cd161feedad6266e34ad95813a1.png)\n\nThe equation of the line $l_{1}$ is $y =\\frac{2}{3}x + 1$ and the line $l_{2}$ is parallel to $l_{1}$. What is the equation of $l_{2}$?$y =$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186481509 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "434017ea-0e77-5f9e-87dd-a52e593bb2fb" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186481509 #edb/ref 1074 "Since parallel lines have equal slopes, the slope of the new line should be equal to the slope of the line\n\n$$\ny = \\frac{2}{3}x + 1\n$$\n\nThe slope of the line\n\n$$\ny = \\frac{2}{3}x + 1\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nSo, we want a line with the slope $\\frac{2}{3}$ that passes through the point $(6,3)$. Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny - y_{1} &= m(x - x_{1}) \\\\\ny - (3) &= \\frac{2}{3}(x - (6)) \\\\\ny &= \\frac{2}{3}x - 4 + (3) \\\\\ny &= \\frac{2}{3}x - 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186481513 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481498 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186481513 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186481513 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/72/72cf21b4c0f68c4b27cd308f6d62003f97ea6f0288e47416df2790b9a5c1a98b.png)\n\nThe equation of the line $l_{1}$ is $y =-\\frac{1}{2}x + 2$ and the line $l_{2}$ is parallel to $l_{1}$. What is the equation of $l_{2}$?$y =$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186481513 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "682fee5b-c01a-52a4-bdef-24527ae261ec" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186481513 #edb/ref 1074 "Since parallel lines have equal slopes, the slope of the new line should be equal to the slope of the line\n\n$$\ny = -\\frac{1}{2}x + 2\n$$\n\nThe slope of the line\n\n$$\ny = -\\frac{1}{2}x + 2\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\n-\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nSo, we want a line with the slope $-\\frac{1}{2}$ that passes through the point $(2,-1)$. Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny - y_{1} &= m(x - x_{1}) \\\\\ny - (-1) &= -\\frac{1}{2}(x - (2)) \\\\\ny &= -\\frac{1}{2}x + 1 + (-1) \\\\\ny &= -\\frac{1}{2}x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186481518 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481529 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186481518 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186481518 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/f8/f873e0b53e0dfa21e5b62089544b548f0426b131ab65002e9f724fb8da78dc72.png)\n\nThe equation of the line $l_{1}$ is $y = -x + 5$ and the line $l_{2}$ is parallel to $l_{1}$. What is the equation of $l_{2}$?" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [1759218