481541 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/db/dbbf49fd68a78969b5b5050f703848a5832bc533e5d2c236389683982901ad9d.png)\n\nThe equation of the line $l_{1}$ is $y = -3x + 5$ and the line $l_{2}$ is parallel to $l_{1}$. What is the equation of $l_{2}$?" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186481541 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f4ec0d10-9ecf-55db-812c-8c2781886239" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186481541 #edb/ref 1074 "Since parallel lines have equal slopes, the slope of the new line should be equal to the slope of the line\n\n$$\ny = -3x + 5\n$$\n\nThe slope of the line\n\n$$\ny = -3x + 5\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\n-3\n$$\n\nSo, we want a line with the slope $-3$ that passes through the point $(0,3)$. Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny - y_{1} &= m(x - x_{1}) \\\\\ny - (3) &= -3(x - (0)) \\\\\ny &= -3x + (3) \\\\\ny &= -3x + 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186481542 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481526 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186481542 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186481542 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/95/95f066686cfcee7d1bbb9c451bd6ea007c8bb5433bee6eda06f886d241280180.png)\n\nThe equation of the line $l_{1}$ is $y = 3x + 3$ and the line $l_{2}$ is parallel to $l_{1}$. What is the equation of $l_{2}$?" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186481542 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f5797ea3-bc2d-51fe-9adc-fde728d4e47d" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186481542 #edb/ref 1074 "Since parallel lines have equal slopes, the slope of the new line should be equal to the slope of the line\n\n$$\ny = 3x + 3\n$$\n\nThe slope of the line\n\n$$\ny = 3x + 3\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\n3\n$$\n\nSo, we want a line with the slope $3$ that passes through the point $(0,1)$. Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny - y_{1} &= m(x - x_{1}) \\\\\ny - (1) &= 3(x - (0)) \\\\\ny &= 3x + (1) \\\\\ny &= 3x + 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [1759218648